  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Rate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></math> reaction is directly dependent on the stability of carbocation intermediate.<br />(II) is primary alkyl halide.<br />(I) is secondary alkyl halide.<br />Primary alkyl halide is less reactive towards <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></math> reaction than secondary alkyl halide.<br />In (III), the carbocation generated is stabilised due to resonance. Hence, (III) is most reactive towards <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></msub><mn>1</mn></math> reaction.</p>",
